The background is dead-right. (Though we do need to talk about the orientation of the board WRT the user. Top-down? ¾? In 3d so that the user can choose?) We're going to have to render these ships. AND! We have to figure out what it looks like when it's looping. (Do we just "crop" it with a fade out?) Focus on the ships in this round. It doesn't need to be photo realistic. It's fine if it is probably cause then we can just cut them out and mask them and place them on top. But I would really like something that is "rendered" but still somehow realistic. Picture what you could do in GL programmatically but have it still look incredible. 

Focus on the "ships" or whatever we're thinking of doing. I want you to generate in either SVG or any JS library (i.e. as an application). Give me a bunch of options. 

<agent-execution agent-id="5BmKo6FjFmGGT9rfdr1R" execution-id="LyfXdz6x9C0hwYps7JAi" name="Code Execution and Data Viz V2" state="complete" usage-record-id="e9c77ab5835cda7a3932878f1276b95b" user-id="oIAIJz43eqWLDyUxW7CMOYnj0Md2"></agent-execution><agent-reason id="3uC4n2KbTq" provider="anthropic" reasoning-id="TOffmQ8KwAKMsXomDJKa" status="completed" signature="" reasoning="%5B%22%22%5D"></agent-reason>Alright Rob, let me push on this. The reference image nails the "operations table" aesthetic — kraft/parchment base, thin hex lines, real objects on top. The interesting tension is that we're going **programmatic-realistic**, not photo. So I'll lean into things that GL/SVG can do *natively* (lighting, gradients, geometry) rather than trying to fake photography.

Before I drop the comparison app, let me name the design axes I'm pulling on, because the choice between these isn't just "which looks coolest" — each one implies a different camera, a different render pipeline, and a different answer to your wrap-edge question:
| Axis | Endpoints |
|----|----|
| **Camera** | Pure top-down ↔ ¾ isometric ↔ orbit-able 3D |
| **Fidelity** | Pictogram (NATO counter) ↔ Silhouette ↔ Detailed model |
| **Aesthetic** | "Drawn on the map" (matches blueprint) ↔ "Resting on the map" (contrast against blueprint) |
| **Renderer** | SVG (cheap, crisp, 2D-only) ↔ Canvas2D ↔ WebGL/Three.js (real lighting, real rotation) |


Top-down + WebGL is the sweet spot for your "GL programmatically but looks incredible" instinct, because top-down lets you cheat 3D modeling massively (you only need to see the deck) while still getting real shadows and depth-of-field. I'll show that one alongside SVG-only options so you can feel the tradeoff.

