### What I did with the architecture

The separation you asked for, in one picture:

``` d2rJO9vhs9
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  DATA MODEL       fleet[], state{}                  │  ← pure data
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  HEX GEOMETRY     axialToPx, hexCorners, shipCells  │  ← pure math
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  buildShipFeatures(ship)  →  { hullPath, deckPath,  │  ← single source
│                                turrets, bridge, … }  │    of ship anatomy
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  renderers.A(g, ship, feat)  ← filled dark           │
│  renderers.B(g, ship, feat)  ← gradient + details   │  ← style only
│  renderers.E(g, ship, feat)  ← outlines              │
│  renderers.F(g, ship, feat)  ← NATO counter (own)   │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  renderBoard()    layered SVG pipeline:              │
│    1. grid  →  2. cell highlights  →  3. ships       │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```


To swap a renderer it's literally `state.render = "A"` then `renderBoard()`. To add a new render mode "G", you write `renderers.G(g, ship, feat)` and add one button.

A and E are now exactly what you described: B's anatomy with different "ink." A is just the hull silhouette with a deck centerline + bow wedge so the front is unambiguous. E is the same shapes drawn as outlines on parchment.

### What I did with the board

* **Highlighted cells under each ship** (blue, like the reference photo). The active ship gets a darker, opaque highlight; the others get a paler one so you can see them all but know which one you're editing.
* **Sidebar workflow**: click a ship card to make it active → use the size / q / r sliders + heading buttons inside the expanded card to move it. The heading buttons re-label themselves automatically when you switch hex orientation.
* **Hex orientation toggle** in the sidebar so you can A/B pointy-top vs flat-top before we lock it. I left pointy-top as default since it matches the reference image's vertical edges.
* **Debug toggles**: hex coords (handy for confirming wrap math later), compass, labels.

### A couple of things I noticed while building it

1. **Pointy-top has a quirk.** Headings 0/180 (E/W) move along a *horizontal* row, so the ship sits flat — looks great. But headings 60/120/240/300 are *diagonal*, and the ship's body bridges cells that are offset by half a hex vertically. The silhouette still reads correctly because the centroid math handles it, but you'll see the hull sitting at a slight tilt across staggered rows. **With flat-top, three of the six headings (NE/SE/N or NW/SW/S depending on which) are axis-aligned vertically, but none are horizontal.** Flip the toggle and you'll see it. I have a mild preference for pointy-top because *east/west reads as "sailing"* — a horizontally-oriented ship feels nautical.
2. **The size slider for each ship** is in there because you asked, but our spec says fixed 5/4/3/3/2. I'd treat the size slider as a *design-time tool* (us iterating) and remove it for the actual game. Easy to gate.
3. **Wrap isn't visualized yet.** If you slide a ship near an edge, the off-board cells just don't highlight. That's deliberately the next study — I want to do the wrap rendering as its own focused round (your "fade vs crop vs ghost" question deserves its own iframe with side-by-side comparisons).

### What to look at / decide

* **Render mode** — flip through A / B / E / F. Does B still feel right as the default, or does E feel more on-brand given the blueprint aesthetic?
* **Orientation** — flip pointy ↔ flat. The diagonal headings will look very different. Which feels more "naval"?
* **Highlight intensity** — too saturated? The reference photo's blue is quite muted. I can dial it down.
* **Ship card UX** — is the click-to-expand pattern working, or would you rather see all controls always visible (more scrolling but no clicks)?

Once these are locked, the next iterations I'd queue up are: (1) wrap-edge rendering study, (2) attacker view with hit/miss footprints and trajectory ruler, (3) defender placement UI with the 10-turn preview from the spec.


